    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Stage 2: measure, then fix",
      "body": [
        "Before cleaning, **count the problems**: the report needs to say how much data was dropped and why. Then clean in a fixed order:",
        "",
        "1. drop exact duplicate rows,",
        "2. tidy text: strip spaces, consistent capitals,",
        "3. turn `\"$1,200.00\"` into `1200.0`,",
        "4. parse dates with an explicit `format=` and `errors=\"coerce\"`, so `2024-02-30` becomes `NaT` instead of crashing,",
        "5. drop rows you cannot use (no date or no quantity),",
        "6. add `revenue = quantity * unit_price`.",
        "",
        "`clean` returns a **new** DataFrame. Never edit the raw data in place: you want to be able to rerun from the start."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "import pandas as pd",
        "",
        "s = pd.Series([\"2024-01-31\", \"2024-02-30\", \"oops\"])",
        "d = pd.to_datetime(s, format=\"%Y-%m-%d\", errors=\"coerce\")",
        "print(d.isna().tolist())",
        "p = pd.Series([\"$25.00\", \"$1,200.00\", \"8.5\"])",
        "print(pd.to_numeric(p.str.replace(\"$\", \"\", regex=False).str.replace(\",\", \"\", regex=False)).tolist())"
      ],
      "answer": [
        "[False, True, True]",
        "[25.0, 1200.0, 8.5]"
      ],
      "explain": "`errors=\"coerce\"` turns anything that is not a real date into `NaT`. Removing `$` and `,` leaves text `to_numeric` can parse."
    },
